  "text": "Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy inside the chloroplasts of plant cells. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue wavelengths while reflecting green light, which is why healthy leaves appear green. The absorbed energy drives two coupled stages: the light reactions split water molecules and release oxygen, and the Calvin cycle fixes carbon dioxide into sugars. Because the two stages operate on different timescales, a plant can adjust to changing light without rebuilding its molecular machinery.\n\nThe printing press spread through Europe in the second half of the fifteenth century. A single workshop could produce hundreds of identical copies of a book in the time a scribe needed for one, and the price of printed matter fell sharply. Printers clustered in cities with paper mills and reliable trade routes, and their output fed the growing market for calendars, pamphlets, and scientific tables. Historians still argue about how quickly the new medium changed reading habits, but the direction of the change is not in dispute.\n\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n    intervals = sorted(intervals, key=lambda pair: pair[0])\n    merged = [list(intervals[0])]\n    for start, end in intervals[1:]:\n        if start <= merged[-1][1]:\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)\n        else:\n            merged.append([start, end])\n    return [tuple(pair) for pair in merged]\n",
